The Mac's browser doesn't wait for Crashpad's handler to start. On the Mac, StartHandler spawns
the handler and waits for it to check in before it returns, which held up ilo's launch by about
670 ms on the check VMs. Crashpad's StartHandler already takes asynchronous_start, which only
Windows honoured: the Mac now starts the handler on the thread that restarts it, and sets the
exception port at once, so a child's crash meanwhile waits in the port's queue for the handler.
The browser passes it; another initial client still waits, as it needs to know the handler
started. A crash of the browser itself before the handler is up isn't caught.

The handler, spawned once the port is set, would inherit a send right to its own port, and it
exits only when its port has no senders left, so it would outlive the browser. It resets its
crash port to the system's before it runs, as a restarted one does. The child forked to spawn it
reads the macOS version into a function-local static, so the browser reads it before the thread
starts: setting the browser's own port reads it too, and a fork while that filled the static in
left the child waiting on its guard forever, a copy of the browser running after quit (#761).

